"---\nname: mba-long-sentences\ndescription: Teach English sentences and paragraphs with 海归湾国际教育's MBA (Minus Before Add, 先减后加) method. Use when a reader asks to 拆句、分析长难句、换词、找骨架 or to break down a sentence. Teach a slow pass with C1+ vocabulary simplified, restore the exact original, replay it quickly, then let the learner try.\n---\n\n# 英语长难句分析智能体 · MBA · Minus Before Add · 先减后加\n\nMethod by Turtle Bay International Education, Tianjin, China. Read `references/signals.md` for grammar decisions and `references/example.md` for a complete lesson.\n\nUse brief Chinese explanations by default. When the learner chooses English, explain in English and retain the requested Chinese translations. Split a paragraph into its sentences without breaking initials, abbreviations or decimals. Give each sentence its own lesson. Preserve its words, spelling, case, punctuation and reading order.\n\n## Teaching sequence\n\nUse the following numbered headings exactly. There is one **unnumbered** transition titled **原句** between 6 and 7. In a chat, show each chunk or substitution on its own line so the order is clear. On screen, animate one change at a time. Let the reader click the named next action or press Enter. Stages 3–4 and 8–9 share one view. As subtraction finishes, the complete core emerges automatically in the center, with its own Chinese translation. Wait there for the learner to start rebuilding. Keep the numbered stages available for review.\n\n| Step | What the learner sees |\n|---|---|\n| 1 · 切割 | Present the original and its word and full-stop counts. Press Enter at its punctuation boundaries so the whole sentence stays on one page. Keep punctuation with the words and keep meaningful apostrophes and hyphens inside words. A sentence without internal punctuation stays on one line, with natural wrapping for the available width. |\n| 2 · 换词 | Keep the larger punctuation blocks from step 1 intact. Within each block, replace a C1-or-higher word or expression with easier English, one occurrence at a time. For each actual swap, show the original, replacement and a short Chinese gloss. Leave all other text intact. Where none qualifies, keep the sentence and say “没有需要替换的 C1+ 词语。” |\n| 3 · 减法 | Begin with the same large blocks. Slowly subdivide them one grammatical boundary at a time, keeping the surrounding words in place. Pair each newly cut English chunk with its own Chinese rendering as soon as it appears. At each modifier signal, press Enter, open a round bracket for a phrase or a square bracket for a clause, and indent nested chunks. Leave visible space between a bracket and the nearest word. Use indentation to show where a nested modifier belongs and where its parent resumes. Keep required complements intact. Briefly explain in Chinese what signalled the cut, exactly which word or action the chunk describes and why. Mark a skeleton line ✓ keep with its role. Identify a clause's inner skeleton and any understood relative pronoun. |\n| 4 · 骨架 | Let the modifiers recede from the entire sentence. Bring the shortest grammatical core into the center of the same view and show its Chinese rendering. Keep both visible without requiring the learner to scroll. Keep the complete predicate pattern: auxiliary, lexical verb, negation when present, required objects and complements. Preserve the meaning of the main action. Show the word count and the relevant questions: 谁、做了什么、对谁、要谁做什么. |\n| 5 · 加法 | Rebuild slowly. Restore modifiers one at a time at their original location in reading order, including small modifiers shortened within the retained lines. As each English chunk returns, display its Chinese translation alongside it. Keep grammar explanations in step 3. Once the sentence is whole, show the full simplified English and its natural Chinese translation together. |\n| 6 · 翻译 | After the simplified English is whole, give a natural, plain Chinese translation of its full meaning. Convey relationships, uncertainty and tone. Split the Chinese into several sentences if that reads better. |\n| 原句 | Undo the step-2 replacements one by one inside the same punctuation blocks. Reveal the exact original wording, including its punctuation. When nothing was changed, simply present the original. |\n| 7 · 切割 | Quickly break the exact original at the same punctuation boundaries used in step 1. |\n| 8 · 减法 | Quickly mark the modifier cuts in the original vocabulary. Keep the same bracket types, order and nesting. Keep the Chinese translation alongside each newly cut English chunk during this faster pass. |\n| 9 · 骨架 | Bring the original-wording core into focus where the learner has been watching the cuts. Show its Chinese translation, required verb pattern and word count. |\n| 10 · 加法 | Add the original chunks back briskly in reading order, one at a time. The final English must match the source. Restore the Chinese translation alongside each addition and show the full translation when the English sentence is complete. |\n| 11 · 翻译 | Give a brief natural Chinese translation of the complete original. Keep its meaning and tone. |\n| 12 · 练习 | Label the screen “Your turn · 轮到你了” and show the complete original again. Invite the learner to try the process independently, with the option to revisit any step. Leave the exercise ungraded and provide no feedback or answer key here. |\n\n## Vocabulary and source alignment\n\nJudge the word's meaning **here** and treat an idiom as a unit. Prefer sense-specific Cambridge learner dictionary or English Vocabulary Profile levels, then Oxford learner wordlists where applicable. Treat an unlabelled specialist term or idiom as C1+ only when there is a sound contextual basis for that judgement. Do not present an editorial estimate as a dictionary-certified level. Keep common and uncertain words. A technical noun can stay with a short Chinese gloss when simpler substitutes would change the thing being named.\n\nKeep tense, agreement, part of speech and verb patterns. Only swap chunks that fit the original slot without creating another grammatical cut. Do not rewrite an entire clause to simplify a single word. Track each occurrence separately, record its exact original span, and check all other characters stayed unchanged. Map every simplified chunk to the exact corresponding original span, punctuation included. In structured output use `text`, `originalText` and a short `zh` for every chunk; use zero-based character offsets for swap and attachment anchors.\n\n## Before finishing\n\nRead the main predicate with its objects and complements intact. Check that the modifier questions point to real words. Rejoin the simplified chunks and compare with step 2, then rejoin the original chunks and compare with the source. Check both whole-sentence Chinese translations and every step-5 chunk gloss. A simple sentence can have an empty replacement list and an empty modifier list. Keep those teaching steps brief and preserve the practice opportunity.\n\nMBA · Minus Before Add — 海归湾国际教育 Turtle Bay International Education\n\n\n# Cut signals · 下刀信号\n\nA modifier describes another word: which one, where, when, how, why, what kind.\nEvery modifier begins with a **signal**. At the signal, start a new indented line with an opening bracket and a space before the word. Keep the bracket open while its modifier continues.\n\n| family | signal (where the bracket opens) | Examples | job |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| adjectives & adverbs | (small, just skip them; no line needed) | a **red** apple · something **cold** · speaks **slowly** · **very** cold | which one / how |\n| 1 prepositional phrase `(` | a preposition: in on at with by for through between of… | a letter **( on the table** · They play football **( in the park** · He made it **( through hard work** | which one · where · when · how · why |\n| 2 -ing phrase `(` | an -ing word right after a noun, or at the very front | a baby **( crying for milk** · The man **( wearing a black jacket** / is my uncle · **( Walking in the park,** / I saw a big bird | which one · when |\n| 3 -ed phrase `(` | an -ed / past participle right after a noun, or at the front | a letter **( written in English** · The photos **( taken in Beijing** / are very nice · **( Tired after school,** / he went to bed early | which one · why |\n| 4 to + verb `(` | *to* right after a noun / pronoun, or after a finished action | a long way **( to go** · something **( to drink** · I went to the kitchen **( to get some water** | what kind · why (purpose) |\n| 5 attributive clause `[` | a noun, then who / which / that / whose / where / when / preposition + which | The boy **[ who sits next to me** / is twelve · the girl **[ whose mother is a doctor** · the school **[ where I study** · the day **[ when we don't go to school** · the support **[ on which the weakest people depend** | which one |\n| 5b zero relative `[` | **no signal word**: a noun followed straight away by a new subject | The sandwich **[ I made** / is for you · The house **[ we live in** / is very old | which one |\n| 5c non-restrictive `[` | comma + who / which | My aunt, **[ who lives in Shanghai,** / often visits us | extra information |\n| 6 adverbial clause `[` | if, because, although, when, before, after, so…that, as, while | **[ If it rains,** / we will stay at home · He stayed home **[ because he was ill** · It was so cold **[ that the water became ice** | when · why · condition · result · contrast |\n| adjective phrase `(` | an adjective after a noun + comma | young people, **( afraid that their future is getting worse** | how they feel |\n\n## Never cut these (✓ keep)\n\n- **Subjects and objects** are never modifiers. *I want to go abroad*: \"to go abroad\" is what I want, the object. It modifies nothing.\n- **Anything that finishes the verb** (predicative): *The letter is on the table* · *The game was exciting* · *I am bored*. Take them away and the sentence breaks.\n- **Verb patterns**: *push / ask / tell / help / allow someone **to do** something*, *make someone **do***, *spend time **doing***, *depend **on***. The second part belongs to the verb.\n- **That-clauses after say / think / know / believe / feel / warn** are objects, not modifiers: *She said **that she was tired*** — keep it.\n- **Adjective + its own phrase** stays together inside one chunk: *afraid that their future is getting worse*, *good at swimming*.\n\n## Quick tests\n- **Cover test**: cover everything in brackets. Still a full sentence? The cuts were right.\n- **Question test**: every bracket must answer which one / where / when / how / why / what kind about one named word.\n- **Clause test**: does the chunk have its own subject + verb? Square bracket. No? Round bracket.\n\n## C1+ vocabulary decisions\n\nCompare the expression's meaning in context. Cambridge lists the pattern *prompt someone to do something* as C2, *soar* (rise quickly) as C2, *prospects* (future chances) as B2 and *fade* (gradually lose strength, including hopes) as B2. Thus the Europe lesson changes *prompted* and *soaring*, but keeps *their prospects are fading*. In the other worked examples, Cambridge lists *coastal*, *estimate* and *bakery* as B2 and *previously* as B1. Those stay.\n\nOxford lists *electoral*, *provision* and *vulnerable* at C1. Specialist fiscal senses and figurative expressions such as *defray*, *anaemic growth*, *entrenched entitlements* and *water down* can also warrant simplification on teaching grounds. These are contextual judgements when the dictionary supplies no level. An unlabelled entry alone is insufficient evidence. Keep a technical term when a putative substitute would change its meaning or grammar; *ice sheets* can be glossed 冰盖 within its own chunk.\n\nReference entries: [Cambridge: prompt someone to do something](https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/prompt-to), [soar](https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/soar), [prospect](https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/prospect), [fade](https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/fade), [coastal](https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/coastal), [estimate](https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/estimate), [previously](https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/previously), [bakery](https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/bakery), [defray](https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/defray), [water down](https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/water-down), [Oxford 5000](https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/external/pdf/wordlists/oxford-3000-5000/The_Oxford_5000.pdf).\n\n\n# Worked example · 欧洲公共预算\n\n## 1 · 切割\n\n61 个词，1 个句号。先在标点后回车，整句放在同一页。\n\n```\nStretched public budgets,\nsqueezed between soaring debt costs and anaemic growth,\nhave prompted growing numbers of young people,\nfearful that their prospects are fading,\nto lash out in countries where cash-strapped governments,\nstruggling to keep their electoral promises,\ndefray the rising cost of entrenched entitlements by incrementally watering down the social provisions on which the most vulnerable have come to depend.\n```\n\n## 2 · 换词\n\n保留第一步的七个标点块，按出现顺序在块内替换，每次换一处。\n\n| 原词 | 换成 | 这里的意思 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stretched | Tight | 财政紧张的 |\n| squeezed | caught | 受到双面挤压 |\n| soaring | fast-growing | 飞涨的 |\n| anaemic | weak | 疲弱的 |\n| prompted | pushed | 促使 |\n| fearful | afraid | 担心的 |\n| lash out | fight back | 奋起反抗 |\n| cash-strapped | poor | 手头拮据的 |\n| electoral | election | 竞选的 |\n| defray | pay | 支付 |\n| entrenched entitlements | old benefits | 沿袭已久的福利待遇 |\n| incrementally | slowly | 逐步地 |\n| watering down | cutting | 削弱、削减 |\n| social provisions | social help | 社会保障 |\n| the most vulnerable | the weakest | 最缺乏保障的人 |\n\n> Tight public budgets, caught between fast-growing debt costs and weak growth, have pushed growing numbers of young people, afraid that their prospects are fading, to fight back in countries where poor governments, struggling to keep their election promises, pay the rising cost of old benefits by slowly cutting the social help on which the weakest have come to depend.\n\n## 3 · 减法\n\n先看完整标点块，再依次展开下面各行。\n\n| 英文块 | 同步中文 |\n|---|---|\n| Tight public budgets, | 紧张的公共预算 |\n| &emsp;(&nbsp; caught between fast-growing debt costs and weak growth, | 债务开支飞涨，经济增长又乏力，两头挤压 |\n| have pushed | 促使 |\n| growing numbers of young people, | 越来越多的年轻人 |\n| &emsp;(&nbsp; afraid that their prospects are fading, | 他们担心自己的前途越来越暗淡 |\n| to fight back | 奋起反抗 |\n| &emsp;(&nbsp; in countries | 在一些国家 |\n| &emsp;&emsp;[&nbsp; where poor governments, | 这些国家的政府手头拮据 |\n| &emsp;&emsp;&emsp;(&nbsp; struggling to keep their election promises, | 正竭力兑现竞选承诺 |\n| &emsp;&emsp;pay the rising cost of old benefits | 承担着维持既有福利待遇所需的、越来越高的开支 |\n| &emsp;&emsp;&emsp;(&nbsp; by slowly cutting the social help | 为此逐步削减社会保障 |\n| &emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;[&nbsp; on which the weakest have come to depend. | 那些最缺乏保障的人正靠它生活 |\n\n| 行 | 信号与理由 / 保留成分 | 修饰谁 | 问什么 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | ✓ 主语。tight、public 都在名词前，可以直接读过。 | — | — |\n| 2 | caught · 分词紧跟名词 | budgets | 为什么预算紧张？ |\n| 3 | ✓ 谓语 | — | — |\n| 4 | ✓ 宾语。push 谁？核心是 young people。 | — | — |\n| 5 | afraid · 逗号后的形容词短语 | young people | 年轻人有什么感受？ |\n| 6 | ✓ 补足动词。push someone to do：促使某人做什么。 | — | — |\n| 7 | in · 介词引出地点 | fight back | 在哪里反抗？ |\n| 8 | where · 关系副词后有主语和谓语 | countries | 哪些国家？ |\n| 9 | struggling · 紧跟 governments 的 -ing 分词，补充政府正在做的事。 | governments | 政府正努力做什么？ |\n| 10 | ✓ 从句谓语及宾语。主语 governments 接回谓语 pay。 | — | — |\n| 11 | by · 介词 by 引出办法 | pay | 怎样支付这些费用？ |\n| 12 | on which · 介词 + 关系代词 | help | 哪些保障？ |\n\n第 8–10 行也有自己的主干：governments pay the cost。第 6 行的 to fight back 补全 push someone to do，必须保留。\n\n## 4 · 骨架\n\n**Budgets have pushed young people to fight back.**（8 词）\n\n预算压力已促使年轻人起来反抗。\n\n| 谁 | 做了什么 | 对谁 | 促使他们做什么 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Budgets | have pushed | young people | to fight back |\n\n## 5 · 加法\n\n按 Enter 或点「加法」，从骨架开始。每加回一块，同时读它的中文。\n\n1. Tight public<br>\n   紧张的公共\n2. caught between fast-growing debt costs and weak growth,<br>\n   债务开支飞涨，经济增长又乏力，两头挤压\n3. growing numbers of<br>\n   越来越多的\n4. afraid that their prospects are fading,<br>\n   他们担心自己的前途越来越暗淡\n5. in countries<br>\n   在一些国家\n6. where poor governments,<br>\n   这些国家的政府手头拮据\n7. struggling to keep their election promises,<br>\n   正竭力兑现竞选承诺\n8. pay the rising cost of old benefits<br>\n   承担着维持既有福利待遇所需的、越来越高的开支\n9. by slowly cutting the social help<br>\n   为此逐步削减社会保障\n10. on which the weakest have come to depend.<br>\n   那些最缺乏保障的人正靠它生活\n\n> Tight public budgets, caught between fast-growing debt costs and weak growth, have pushed growing numbers of young people, afraid that their prospects are fading, to fight back in countries where poor governments, struggling to keep their election promises, pay the rising cost of old benefits by slowly cutting the social help on which the weakest have come to depend.\n\n## 6 · 翻译\n\n各国政府的预算本来就紧，债务开支还在猛涨，经济增长又乏力。为了兑现竞选承诺、承担越来越高的既有福利开支，一些政府只好逐步削减最缺乏保障的人赖以生活的社会支持。眼看前途越来越暗淡，越来越多的年轻人起来反抗。\n\n## 原句\n\n把词一个个还原，每次只改变对应的那一处。\n\n1. Tight → Stretched\n2. caught → squeezed\n3. fast-growing → soaring\n4. weak → anaemic\n5. pushed → prompted\n6. afraid → fearful\n7. fight back → lash out\n8. poor → cash-strapped\n9. election → electoral\n10. pay → defray\n11. old benefits → entrenched entitlements\n12. slowly → incrementally\n13. cutting → watering down\n14. social help → social provisions\n15. the weakest → the most vulnerable\n\n> Stretched public budgets, squeezed between soaring debt costs and anaemic growth, have prompted growing numbers of young people, fearful that their prospects are fading, to lash out in countries where cash-strapped governments, struggling to keep their electoral promises, defray the rising cost of entrenched entitlements by incrementally watering down the social provisions on which the most vulnerable have come to depend.\n\n## 7 · 切割\n\n```\nStretched public budgets,\nsqueezed between soaring debt costs and anaemic growth,\nhave prompted growing numbers of young people,\nfearful that their prospects are fading,\nto lash out in countries where cash-strapped governments,\nstruggling to keep their electoral promises,\ndefray the rising cost of entrenched entitlements by incrementally watering down the social provisions on which the most vulnerable have come to depend.\n```\n\n## 8 · 减法\n\n先看完整标点块，再依次展开下面各行。\n\n| 英文块 | 同步中文 |\n|---|---|\n| Stretched public budgets, | 紧张的公共预算 |\n| &emsp;(&nbsp; squeezed between soaring debt costs and anaemic growth, | 债务开支飞涨，经济增长又乏力，两头挤压 |\n| have prompted | 促使 |\n| growing numbers of young people, | 越来越多的年轻人 |\n| &emsp;(&nbsp; fearful that their prospects are fading, | 他们担心自己的前途越来越暗淡 |\n| to lash out | 奋起反抗 |\n| &emsp;(&nbsp; in countries | 在一些国家 |\n| &emsp;&emsp;[&nbsp; where cash-strapped governments, | 这些国家的政府手头拮据 |\n| &emsp;&emsp;&emsp;(&nbsp; struggling to keep their electoral promises, | 正竭力兑现竞选承诺 |\n| &emsp;&emsp;defray the rising cost of entrenched entitlements | 承担着维持既有福利待遇所需的、越来越高的开支 |\n| &emsp;&emsp;&emsp;(&nbsp; by incrementally watering down the social provisions | 为此逐步削减社会保障 |\n| &emsp;&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;[&nbsp; on which the most vulnerable have come to depend. | 那些最缺乏保障的人正靠它生活 |\n\n## 9 · 骨架\n\n**Budgets have prompted young people to lash out.**（8 词）\n\n预算压力已促使年轻人愤而反击。\n\n## 10 · 加法\n\n1. Stretched public<br>\n   紧张的公共\n2. squeezed between soaring debt costs and anaemic growth,<br>\n   债务开支飞涨，经济增长又乏力，两头挤压\n3. growing numbers of<br>\n   越来越多的\n4. fearful that their prospects are fading,<br>\n   他们担心自己的前途越来越暗淡\n5. in countries<br>\n   在一些国家\n6. where cash-strapped governments,<br>\n   这些国家的政府手头拮据\n7. struggling to keep their electoral promises,<br>\n   正竭力兑现竞选承诺\n8. defray the rising cost of entrenched entitlements<br>\n   承担着维持既有福利待遇所需的、越来越高的开支\n9. by incrementally watering down the social provisions<br>\n   为此逐步削减社会保障\n10. on which the most vulnerable have come to depend.<br>\n   那些最缺乏保障的人正靠它生活\n\n> Stretched public budgets, squeezed between soaring debt costs and anaemic growth, have prompted growing numbers of young people, fearful that their prospects are fading, to lash out in countries where cash-strapped governments, struggling to keep their electoral promises, defray the rising cost of entrenched entitlements by incrementally watering down the social provisions on which the most vulnerable have come to depend.\n\n## 11 · 翻译\n\n债务开支飙升、经济增长乏力，压得一些政府喘不过气。为了兑现竞选承诺、负担越来越高的既有福利开支，它们逐步削减弱势群体赖以生活的保障。越来越多的年轻人担心前途黯淡，奋起反抗。\n\n## 12 · 练习\n\n**Your turn · 轮到你了**\n\n> Stretched public budgets, squeezed between soaring debt costs and anaemic growth, have prompted growing numbers of young people, fearful that their prospects are fading, to lash out in countries where cash-strapped governments, struggling to keep their electoral promises, defray the rising cost of entrenched entitlements by incrementally watering down the social provisions on which the most vulnerable have come to depend.\n\n现在自己试一次：在哪里切？去掉修饰语，剩下什么？想再看一遍，可以选择任意一步。\n\nMBA · Minus Before Add — 海归湾国际教育 Turtle Bay International Education\n"